    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so with the AC part, why does the current actually keep flowing if the capacitor is technically just charging and discharging? and how does the frequency affect that reactance value specifically? like why does a higher frequency make the reactance lower?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When an AC voltage is applied to a capacitor, a capacitor's charging and discharging are periodic events that align with the frequency of the AC power source. Because of this periodic behavior, the capacitor allows the instantaneous current flow to combine with it, resulting in a continuous AC current flow, as the capacitor responds very quickly to changes in the voltage, acting almost in unison with the alternating current.\n\n\nCapacitive reactance inversely depends on both the frequency and the capacitance. As the frequency of the AC circuit increases, the capacitor has less time to charge before the voltage changes direction, which means the impedance it poses to the AC current decreases. Conversely, at lower frequencies, the capacitor has more time to charge up, so higher resistance in the form of reactance is encountered. Mathematically, since reactance is inversely proportional to frequency, a higher frequency (f) leads to a lower reactance (Xc).",
